" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rust-lang/cargo/rust-1.93.0/src/doc/src/reference/environment-variables.md)\nciteturn63view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rust-lang/cargo/rust-1.93.0/src/doc/src/reference/environment-variables.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 419\nL0: # Environment Variables\nL1: \nL2: Cargo sets and reads a number of environment variables which your code can detect\nL3: or override. Here is a list of the variables Cargo sets, organized by when it interacts\nL4: with them:\nL5: ## Environment variables Cargo reads\nL6: \nL7: You can override these environment variables to change Cargo's behavior on your\nL8: system:\nL9: * `CARGO_LOG` --- Cargo uses the [`tracing`] crate to display debug log messages.\nL10:   The `CARGO_LOG` environment variable can be set to enable debug logging,\nL11:   with a value such as `trace`, `debug`, or `warn`.\nL12:   Usually it is only used during debugging. For more details refer to the\nL13:   [Debug logging].\nL14: * `CARGO_HOME` --- Cargo maintains a local cache of the registry index and of\nL15:   git checkouts of crates. By default these are stored under `$HOME/.cargo`\nL16:   (`%USERPROFILE%\\.cargo` on Windows), but this variable overrides the\nL17:   location of this directory. Once a crate is cached it is not removed by the\nL18:   clean command.\nL19:   For more details refer to the [guide](../guide/cargo-home.md).\nL20: * `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` --- Location of where to place all generated artifacts,\nL21:   relative to the current working directory. See [`build.target-dir`] to set\nL22:   via config.\nL23: * `CARGO` --- If set, Cargo will forward this value instead of setting it\nL24:   to its own auto-detected path when it builds crates and when it\nL25:   executes build scripts and external subcommands. This value is not\nL26:   directly executed by Cargo, and should always point at a command that\nL27:   behaves exactly like `cargo`, as that's what users of the variable\nL28:   will be expecting.\nL29: * `RUSTC` --- Instead of running `rustc`, Cargo will execute this specified\nL30:   compiler instead. See [`build.rustc`] to set via config.\nL31: * `RUSTC_WRAPPER` --- Instead of simply running `rustc`, Cargo will execute this\nL32:   specified wrapper, passing as its command-line arguments the rustc\nL33:   invocation, with the first argument being the path to the actual rustc.\nL34:   Useful to set up a build cache tool such as `sccache`. See\nL35:   [`build.rustc-wrapper`] to set via config. Setting this to the empty string\nL36:   overwrites the config and resets cargo to not use a wrapper.\nL37: * `RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER` --- Instead of simply running `rustc`, for workspace members Cargo will\nL38:   execute this specified wrapper, passing as its command-line arguments the rustc invocation, with\nL39:   the first argument being the path to the actual rustc. When building a single-package project\nL40:   without workspaces, that package is considered to be the workspace. It affects the filename hash\nL41:   so that artifacts produced by the wrapper are cached separately. See\nL42:   [`build.rustc-workspace-wrapper`] to set via config. Setting this to the empty string overwrites\nL43:   the config and resets cargo to not use a wrapper for workspace members. If both `RUSTC_WRAPPER`\nL44:   and `RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER` are set, then they will be nested: the final invocation is\nL45:   `$RUSTC_WRAPPER $RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER $RUSTC`.\nL46: * `RUSTDOC` --- Instead of running `rustdoc`, Cargo will execute this specified\nL47:   `rustdoc` instance instead. See [`build.rustdoc`] to set via config.\nL48: * `RUSTDOCFLAGS` --- A space-separated list of custom flags to pass to all `rustdoc`\nL49:   invocations that Cargo performs. In contrast with [`cargo rustdoc`], this is\nL50:   useful for passing a flag to *all* `rustdoc` instances. See\nL51:   [`build.rustdocflags`] for some more ways to set flags. This string is\nL52:   split by whitespace; for a more robust encoding of multiple arguments,\nL53:   see `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTDOCFLAGS`.\nL54: * `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTDOCFLAGS` ---  A list of custom flags separated by `0x1f`\nL55:   (ASCII Unit Separator) to pass to all `rustdoc` invocations that Cargo performs.\nL56: * `RUSTFLAGS` --- A space-separated list of custom flags to pass to all compiler\nL57:   invocations that Cargo performs. In contrast with [`cargo rustc`], this is\nL58:   useful for passing a flag to *all* compiler instances. See\nL59:   [`build.rustflags`] for some more ways to set flags. This string is\nL60:   split by whitespace; for a more robust encoding of multiple arguments,\nL61:   see `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS`.\nL62: * `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` --- A list of custom flags separated by `0x1f`\nL63:   (ASCII Unit Separator) to pass to all compiler invocations that Cargo performs.\nL64: * `CARGO_INCREMENTAL` --- If this is set to 1 then Cargo will force [incremental\nL65:   compilation] to be enabled for the current compilation, and when set to 0 it\nL66:   will force disabling it. If this env var isn't present then cargo's defaults\nL67:   will otherwise be used. See also [`build.incremental`] config value.\nL68: * `CARGO_CACHE_RUSTC_INFO` --- If this is set to 0 then Cargo will not try to cache\nL69:   compiler version information.\nL70: * `HTTPS_PROXY` or `https_proxy` or `http_proxy` --- The HTTP proxy to use, see\nL71:   [`http.proxy`] for more detail.\nL72: * `HTTP_TIMEOUT` --- The HTTP timeout in seconds, see [`http.timeout`] for more\nL73:   detail.\nL74: * `TERM` --- If this is set to `dumb`, it disables the progress bar.\nL75: * `BROWSER` --- The web browser to execute to open documentation with [`cargo\nL76:   doc`]'s `--open` flag, see [`doc.browser`] for more details.\nL77: * `RUSTFMT` --- Instead of running `rustfmt`,\nL78:   [`cargo fmt`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rustfmt) will execute this specified\nL79:   `rustfmt` instance instead.\nL80: ### Configuration environment variables\nL81: \nL82: Cargo reads environment variables for some configuration values.\nL83: See the [configuration chapter][config-env] for more details.\nL84: In summary, the supported environment variables are:\nL85: * `CARGO_ALIAS_<name>` --- Command aliases, see [`alias`].\nL86: * `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` --- Number of parallel jobs, see [`build.jobs`].\nL87: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC` --- The `rustc` executable, see [`build.rustc`].\nL88: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WRAPPER` --- The `rustc` wrapper, see [`build.rustc-wrapper`].\nL89: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER` --- The `rustc` wrapper for workspace members only, see [`build.rustc-workspace-wrapper`].\nL90: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTDOC` --- The `rustdoc` executable, see [`build.rustdoc`].\nL91: * `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET` --- The default target platform, see [`build.target`].\nL92: * `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR` --- The default output directory, see [`build.target-dir`].\nL93: * `CARGO_BUILD_BUILD_DIR` --- The default build directory, see [`build.build-dir`].\nL94: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS` --- Extra `rustc` flags, see [`build.rustflags`].\nL95: * `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTDOCFLAGS` --- Extra `rustdoc` flags, see [`build.rustdocflags`].\nL96: * `CARGO_BUILD_INCREMENTAL` --- Incremental compilation, see [`build.incremental`].\nL97: * `CARGO_BUILD_DEP_INFO_BASEDIR` --- Dep-info relative directory, see [`build.dep-info-basedir`].\nL98: * `CARGO_CACHE_AUTO_CLEAN_FREQUENCY` --- Configures how often automatic cache cleaning runs, see [`cache.auto-clean-frequency`].\nL99: * `CARGO_CARGO_NEW_VCS` --- The default source control system with [`cargo new`], see [`cargo-new.vcs`].\nL100: * `CARGO_FUTURE_INCOMPAT_REPORT_FREQUENCY` --- How often we should generate a future incompat report notification, see [`future-incompat-report.frequency`].\nL101: * `CARGO_HTTP_DEBUG` --- Enables HTTP debugging, see [`http.debug`].\nL102: * `CARGO_HTTP_PROXY` --- Enables HTTP proxy, see [`http.proxy`].\nL103: * `CARGO_HTTP_TIMEOUT` --- The HTTP timeout, see [`http.timeout`].\nL104: * `CARGO_HTTP_CAINFO` --- The TLS certificate Certificate Authority file, see [`http.cainfo`].\nL105: * `CARGO_HTTP_PROXY_CAINFO` --- The proxy TLS certificate Certificate Authority file, see [`http.proxy-cainfo`].\nL106: * `CARGO_HTTP_CHECK_REVOKE` --- Disables TLS certificate revocation checks, see [`http.check-revoke`].\nL107: * `CARGO_HTTP_SSL_VERSION` --- The TLS version to use, see [`http.ssl-version`].\nL108: * `CARGO_HTTP_LOW_SPEED_LIMIT` --- The HTTP low-speed limit, see [`http.low-speed-limit`].\nL109: * `CARGO_HTTP_MULTIPLEXING` --- Whether HTTP/2 multiplexing is used, see [`http.multiplexing`].\nL110: * `CARGO_HTTP_USER_AGENT` --- The HTTP user-agent header, see [`http.user-agent`].\nL111: * `CARGO_INSTALL_ROOT` --- The default directory for [`cargo install`], see [`install.root`].\nL112: * `CARGO_NET_RETRY` --- Number of times to retry network errors, see [`net.retry`].\nL113: * `CARGO_NET_GIT_FETCH_WITH_CLI` --- Enables the use of the `git` executable to fetch, see [`net.git-fetch-with-cli`].\nL114: * `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE` --- Offline mode, see [`net.offline`].\nL115: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_BUILD_OVERRIDE_<key>` --- Override build script profile, see [`profile.<name>.build-override`].\nL116: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_CODEGEN_UNITS` --- Set code generation units, see [`profile.<name>.codegen-units`].\nL117: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_DEBUG` --- What kind of debug info to include, see [`profile.<name>.debug`].\nL118: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_DEBUG_ASSERTIONS` --- Enable/disable debug assertions, see [`profile.<name>.debug-assertions`].\nL119: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_INCREMENTAL` --- Enable/disable incremental compilation, see [`profile.<name>.incremental`].\nL120: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_LTO` --- Link-time optimization, see [`profile.<name>.lto`].\nL121: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_OVERFLOW_CHECKS` --- Enable/disable overflow checks, see [`profile.<name>.overflow-checks`].\nL122: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_OPT_LEVEL` --- Set the optimization level, see [`profile.<name>.opt-level`].\nL123: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_PANIC` --- The panic strategy to use, see [`profile.<name>.panic`].\nL124: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_RPATH` --- The rpath linking option, see [`profile.<name>.rpath`].\nL125: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_SPLIT_DEBUGINFO` --- Controls debug file output behavior, see [`profile.<name>.split-debuginfo`].\nL126: * `CARGO_PROFILE_<name>_STRIP` --- Controls stripping of symbols and/or debuginfos, see [`profile.<name>.strip`].\nL127: * `CARGO_REGISTRIES_<name>_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER` --- Credential provider for a registry, see [`registries.<name>.credential-provider`].\nL128: * `CARGO_REGISTRIES_<name>_INDEX` --- URL of a registry index, see [`registries.<name>.index`].\nL129: * `CARGO_REGISTRIES_<name>_TOKEN` --- Authentication token of a registry, see [`registries.<name>.token`].\nL130: * `CARGO_REGISTRY_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER` --- Credential provider for [crates.io], see [`registry.credential-provider`].\nL131: * `CARGO_REGISTRY_DEFAULT` --- Default registry for the `--registry` flag, see [`registry.default`].\nL132: * `CARGO_REGISTRY_GLOBAL_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDERS` --- Credential providers for registries that do not have a specific provider defined. See [`registry.global-credential-providers`].\nL133: * `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` --- Authentication token for [crates.io], see [`registry.token`].\nL134: * `CARGO_TARGET_<triple>_LINKER` --- The linker to use, see [`target.<triple>.linker`]. The triple must be [converted to uppercase and underscores](config.md#environment-variables).\nL135: * `CARGO_TARGET_<triple>_RUNNER` --- The executable runner, see [`target.<triple>.runner`].\nL136: * `CARGO_TARGET_<triple>_RUSTFLAGS` --- Extra `rustc` flags for a target, see [`target.<triple>.rustflags`].\nL137: * `CARGO_TERM_QUIET` --- Quiet mode, see [`term.quiet`].\nL138: * `CARGO_TERM_VERBOSE` --- The default terminal verbosity, see [`term.verbose`].\nL139: * `CARGO_TERM_COLOR` --- The default color mode, see [`term.color`].\nL140: * `CARGO_TERM_PROGRESS_WHEN` --- The default progress bar showing mode, see [`term.progress.when`].\nL141: * `CARGO_TERM_PROGRESS_WIDTH` --- The default progress bar width, see [`term.progress.width`].\nL142: [`cargo doc`]: ../commands/cargo-doc.md\nL143: [`cargo install`]: ../commands/cargo-install.md\nL144: [`cargo new`]: ../commands/cargo-new.md\nL145: [`cargo rustc`]: ../commands/cargo-rustc.md\nL146: [`cargo rustdoc`]: ../commands/cargo-rustdoc.md\nL147: [config-env]: config.md#environment-variables\nL148: [crates.io]: https://crates.io/\nL149: [incremental compilation]: profiles.md#incremental\nL150: [`alias`]: config.md#alias\nL151: [`build.jobs`]: config.md#buildjobs\nL152: [`build.rustc`]: config.md#buildrustc\nL153: [`build.rustc-wrapper`]: config.md#buildrustc-wrapper\nL154: [`build.rustc-workspace-wrapper`]: config.md#buildrustc-workspace-wrapper\nL155: [`build.rustdoc`]: config.md#buildrustdoc\nL156: [`build.target`]: config.md#buildtarget\nL157: [`build.target-dir`]: config.md#buildtarget-dir\nL158: [`build.build-dir`]: config.md#buildbuild-dir\nL159: [`build.rustflags`]: config.md#buildrustflags\nL160: [`build.rustdocflags`]: config.md#buildrustdocflags\nL161: [`build.incremental`]: config.md#buildincremental\nL162: [`build.dep-info-basedir`]: config.md#builddep-info-basedir\nL163: [`doc.browser`]: config.md#docbrowser\nL164: [`cache.auto-clean-frequency`]: config.md#cacheauto-clean-frequency\nL165: [`cargo-new.name`]: config.md#cargo-newname\nL166: [`cargo-new.email`]: config.md#cargo-newemail\nL167: [`cargo-new.vcs`]: config.md#cargo-newvcs\nL168: [`future-incompat-report.frequency`]: config.md#future-incompat-reportfrequency\nL169: [`http.debug`]: config.md#httpdebug\nL170: [`http.proxy`]: config.md#httpproxy\nL171: [`http.timeout`]: config.md#httptimeout\nL172: [`http.cainfo`]: config.md#httpcainfo\nL173: [`http.proxy-cainfo`]: config.md#httpproxy-cainfo\nL174: [`http.check-revoke`]: config.md#httpcheck-revoke\nL175: [`http.ssl-version`]: config.md#httpssl-version\nL176: [`http.low-speed-limit`]: config.md#httplow-speed-limit\nL177: [`http.multiplexing`]: config.md#httpmultiplexing\nL178: [`http.user-agent`]: config.md#httpuser-agent\nL179: [`install.root`]: config.md#installroot\nL180: [`net.retry`]: config.md#netretry\nL181: [`net.git-fetch-with-cli`]: config.md#netgit-fetch-with-cli\nL182: [`net.offline`]: config.md#netoffline\nL183: [`profile.<name>.build-override`]: config.md#profilenamebuild-override\nL184: [`profile.<name>.codegen-units`]: config.md#profilenamecodegen-units\nL185: [`profile.<name>.debug`]: config.md#profilenamedebug\nL186: [`profile.<name>.debug-assertions`]: config.md#profilenamedebug-assertions\nL187: [`profile.<name>.incremental`]: config.md#profilenameincremental\nL188: [`profile.<name>.lto`]: config.md#profilenamelto\nL189: [`profile.<name>.overflow-checks`]: config.md#profilenameoverflow-checks\nL190: [`profile.<name>.opt-level`]: config.md#profilenameopt-level\nL191: [`profile.<name>.panic`]: config.md#profilenamepanic\nL192: [`profile.<name>.rpath`]: config.md#profilenamerpath\nL193: [`profile.<name>.split-debuginfo`]: config.md#profilenamesplit-debuginfo\nL194: [`profile.<name>.strip`]: config.md#profilenamestrip\nL195: [`registries.<name>.credential-provider`]: config.md#registriesnamecredential-provider\nL196: [`registries.<name>.index`]: config.md#registriesnameindex\nL197: [`registries.<name>.token`]: config.md#registriesnametoken\nL198: [`registry.credential-provider`]: config.md#registrycredential-provider\nL199: [`registry.default`]: config.md#registrydefault\nL200: [`registry.global-credential-providers`]: config.md#registryglobal-credential-providers\nL201: [`registry.token`]: config.md#registrytoken\nL202: [`target.<triple>.linker`]: config.md#targettriplelinker\nL203: [`target.<triple>.runner`]: config.md#targettriplerunner\nL204: [`target.<triple>.rustflags`]: config.md#targettriplerustflags\nL205: [`term.quiet`]: config.md#termquiet\nL206: [`term.verbose`]: config.md#termverbose\nL207: [`term.color`]: config.md#termcolor\nL208: [`term.progress.when`]: config.md#termprogresswhen\nL209: [`term.progress.width`]: config.md#termprogresswidth\nL210: ## Environment variables Cargo sets for crates\nL211: \nL212: Cargo exposes these environment variables to your crate when it is compiled.\nL213: Note that this applies for running binaries with `cargo run` and `cargo test`\nL214: as well. To get the value of any of these variables in a Rust program, do\nL215: this:\nL216: \nL217: ```rust,ignore\nL218: let version = env!(\"CARGO_PKG_VERSION\");\nL219: ```\nL220: \nL221: `version` will now contain the value of `CARGO_PKG_VERSION`.\nL222: Note that if one of these values is not provided in the manifest, the\nL223: corresponding environment variable is set to the empty string, `\"\"`.\nL224: * `CARGO` --- Path to the `cargo` binary performing the build.\nL225: * `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` --- The directory containing the manifest of your package.\nL226: * `CARGO_MANIFEST_PATH` --- The path to the manifest of your package.\nL227: * `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` --- The full version of your package.\nL228: * `CARGO_PKG_VERSION_MAJOR` --- The major version of your package.\nL229: * `CARGO_PKG_VERSION_MINOR` --- The minor version of your package.\nL230: * `CARGO_PKG_VERSION_PATCH` --- The patch version of your package.\nL231: * `CARGO_PKG_VERSION_PRE` --- The pre-release version of your package.\nL232: * `CARGO_PKG_AUTHORS` --- Colon separated list of authors from the manifest of your package.\nL233: * `CARGO_PKG_NAME` --- The name of your package.\nL234: * `CARGO_PKG_DESCRIPTION` --- The description from the manifest of your package.\nL235: * `CARGO_PKG_HOMEPAGE` --- The home page from the manifest of your package.\nL236: * `CARGO_PKG_REPOSITORY` --- The repository from the manifest of your package.\nL237: * `CARGO_PKG_LICENSE` --- The license from the manifest of your package.\nL238: * `CARGO_PKG_LICENSE_FILE` --- The license file from the manifest of your package.\nL239: * `CARGO_PKG_RUST_VERSION` --- The Rust version from the manifest of your package.\nL240:   Note that this is the minimum Rust version supported by the package, not the\nL241:   current Rust version.\nL242: * `CARGO_PKG_README` --- Path to the README file of your package.\nL243: * `CARGO_CRATE_NAME` --- The name of the crate that is currently being compiled. It is the name of the [Cargo target] with `-` converted to `_`, such as the name of the library, binary, example, integration test, or benchmark.\nL244: * `CARGO_BIN_NAME` --- The name of the binary that is currently being compiled.\nL245:   Only set for [binaries] or binary [examples]. This name does not include any\nL246:   file extension, such as `.exe`.\nL247: * `OUT_DIR` --- If the package has a build script, this is set to the folder\nL248:   where the build script should place its output. See below for more information.\nL249:   (Only set during compilation.)\nL250: * `CARGO_BIN_EXE_<name>` --- The absolute path to a binary target's executable.\nL251:   This is only set when building an [integration test] or benchmark. This may\nL252:   be used with the [`env` macro] to find the executable to run for testing\nL253:   purposes. The `<name>` is the name of the binary target, exactly as-is. For\nL254:   example, `CARGO_BIN_EXE_my-program` for a binary named `my-program`.\nL255:   Binaries are automatically built when the test is built, unless the binary\nL256:   has required features that are not enabled.\nL257: * `CARGO_PRIMARY_PACKAGE` --- This environment variable will be set if the\nL258:   package being built is primary. Primary packages are the ones the user\nL259:   selected on the command-line, either with `-p` flags or the defaults based\nL260:   on the current directory and the default workspace members.\nL261:   This variable will not be set when building dependencies,\nL262:   unless a dependency is also a workspace member that was also selected on the command-line.\nL263:   This is only set when compiling the package (not when running binaries or tests).\nL264: * `CARGO_TARGET_TMPDIR` --- Only set when building [integration test] or benchmark code.\nL265:   This is a path to a directory inside the target directory\nL266:   where integration tests or benchmarks are free to put any data needed by\nL267:   the tests/benches. Cargo initially creates this directory but doesn't\nL268:   manage its content in any way, this is the responsibility of the test code.\nL269: [Cargo target]: cargo-targets.md\nL270: [binaries]: cargo-targets.md#binaries\nL271: [examples]: cargo-targets.md#examples\nL272: [integration test]: cargo-targets.md#integration-tests\nL273: [`env` macro]: ../../std/macro.env.html\nL274: ### Dynamic library paths\nL275: \nL276: Cargo also sets the dynamic library path when compiling and running binaries\nL277: with commands like `cargo run` and `cargo test`. This helps with locating\nL278: shared libraries that are part of the build process. The variable name depends\nL279: on the platform:\nL280: \nL281: * Windows: `PATH`\nL282: * macOS: `DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH`\nL283: * Unix: `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`\nL284: * AIX: `LIBPATH`\nL285: The value is extended from the existing value when Cargo starts. macOS has\nL286: special consideration where if `DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH` is not already\nL287: set, it will add the default `$HOME/lib:/usr/local/lib:/usr/lib`.\nL288: \nL289: Cargo includes the following paths:\nL290: * Search paths included from any build script with the [`rustc-link-search`\nL291:   instruction](build-scripts.md#rustc-link-search). Paths outside of the\nL292:   `target` directory are removed. It is the responsibility of the user running\nL293:   Cargo to properly set the environment if additional libraries on the system\nL294:   are needed in the search path.\nL295: * The base output directory, such as `target/debug`, and the \"deps\" directory.\nL296:   This is mostly for support of proc-macros.\nL297: * The rustc sysroot library path. This generally is not important to most\nL298:   users.\nL299: ## Environment variables Cargo sets for build scripts\nL300: \nL301: Cargo sets several environment variables when build scripts are run. Because these variables\nL302: are not yet set when the build script is compiled, the above example using `env!` won't work\nL303: and instead you'll need to retrieve the values when the build script is run:\nL304: \nL305: ```rust,ignore\nL306: use std::env;\nL307: let out_dir = env::var(\"OUT_DIR\").unwrap();\nL308: ```\nL309: \nL310: `out_dir` will now contain the value of `OUT_DIR`.\nL311: * `CARGO` --- Path to the `cargo` binary performing the build.\nL312: * `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` --- The directory containing the manifest for the package\nL313:   being built (the package containing the build script). Also note that this is\nL314:   the value of the current working directory of the build script when it starts.\nL315: * `CARGO_MANIFEST_PATH` --- The path to the manifest of your package.\nL316: * `CARGO_MANIFEST_LINKS` --- the manifest `links` value.\nL317: * `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS` --- Contains parameters needed for Cargo's [jobserver]\nL318:   implementation to parallelize subprocesses. Rustc or cargo invocations from\nL319:   build.rs can already read `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS`, but GNU Make requires the flags\nL320:   to be specified either directly as arguments, or through the `MAKEFLAGS`\nL321:   environment variable. Currently Cargo doesn't set the `MAKEFLAGS` variable,\nL322:   but it's free for build scripts invoking GNU Make to set it to the contents\nL323:   of `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS`.\nL324: * `CARGO_FEATURE_<name>` --- For each activated feature of the package being built,\nL325:   this environment variable will be present where `<name>` is the name of the\nL326:   feature uppercased and having `-` translated to `_`.\nL327: * `CARGO_CFG_<cfg>` --- For each [configuration option][configuration] of the\nL328:   package being built, this environment variable will contain the value of the\nL329:   configuration, where `<cfg>` is the name of the configuration uppercased and\nL330:   having `-` translated to `_`. Boolean configurations are present if they are\nL331:   set, and not present otherwise. Configurations with multiple values are\nL332:   joined to a single variable with the values delimited by `,`. This includes\nL333:   values built-in to the compiler (which can be seen with `rustc --print=cfg`)\nL334:   and values set by build scripts and extra flags passed to `rustc` (such as\nL335:   those defined in `RUSTFLAGS`). Some examples of what these variables are:\nL336:     * `CARGO_CFG_FEATURE` --- Each activated feature of the package being built.\nL337:     * `CARGO_CFG_UNIX` --- Set on [unix-like platforms].\nL338:     * `CARGO_CFG_WINDOWS` --- Set on [windows-like platforms].\nL339:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_FAMILY=unix,wasm` --- The [target family].\nL340:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS=macos` --- The [target operating system].\nL341:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH=x86_64` --- The CPU [target architecture].\nL342:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_VENDOR=apple` --- The [target vendor].\nL343:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ENV=gnu` --- The [target environment] ABI.\nL344:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ABI=eabihf` --- The [target ABI].\nL345:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_POINTER_WIDTH=64` --- The CPU [pointer width].\nL346:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ENDIAN=little` --- The CPU [target endianness].\nL347:     * `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_FEATURE=mmx,sse` --- List of CPU [target features] enabled.\nL348:   > Note that different [target triples][Target Triple] have different sets of `cfg` values,\nL349:   > hence variables present in one target triple might not be available in the other.\nL350:   >\nL351:   > Some cfg values like `test` are not available.\nL352: * `OUT_DIR` --- the folder in which all output and intermediate artifacts should\nL353:   be placed. This folder is inside the build directory for the package being built,\nL354:   and it is unique for the package in question.\nL355: * `TARGET` --- the target triple that is being compiled for. Native code should be\nL356:   compiled for this triple. See the [Target Triple] description for more information.\nL357: * `HOST` --- the host triple of the Rust compiler.\nL358: * `NUM_JOBS` --- the parallelism specified as the top-level parallelism. This can\nL359:   be useful to pass a `-j` parameter to a system like `make`. Note that care\nL360:   should be taken when interpreting this environment variable. For historical\nL361:   purposes this is still provided but recent versions of Cargo, for example, do\nL362:   not need to run `make -j`, and instead can set the `MAKEFLAGS` env var to the\nL363:   content of `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS` to activate the use of Cargo's GNU Make compatible\nL364:   [jobserver] for sub-make invocations.\nL365: * `OPT_LEVEL`, `DEBUG` --- values of the corresponding variables for the profile currently being built.\nL366: * `PROFILE` --- `release` for release builds, `debug` for other builds. This is\nL367:   determined based on if the [profile] inherits from the [`dev`] or\nL368:   [`release`] profile. Using this environment variable is not recommended.\nL369:   Using other environment variables like `OPT_LEVEL` provide a more correct\nL370:   view of the actual settings being used.\nL371: * `DEP_<links>_<key>` --- For more information about this set of environment variables,\nL372:   see build script documentation about [`links`][links].\nL373: * `RUSTC`, `RUSTDOC` --- the compiler and documentation generator that Cargo has\nL374:   resolved to use, passed to the build script so it might use it as well.\nL375: * `RUSTC_WRAPPER` --- the `rustc` wrapper, if any, that Cargo is using. See [`build.rustc-wrapper`].\nL376: * `RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER` --- the `rustc` wrapper, if any, that Cargo is using\nL377:   for workspace members. See [`build.rustc-workspace-wrapper`].\nL378: * `RUSTC_LINKER` --- The path to the linker binary that Cargo has resolved to use\nL379:   for the current target, if specified. The linker can be changed by editing\nL380:   `.cargo/config.toml`; see the documentation about [cargo configuration][cargo-config]\nL381:   for more information.\nL382: * `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` --- extra flags that Cargo invokes `rustc` with,\nL383:   separated by a `0x1f` character (ASCII Unit Separator). See\nL384:   [`build.rustflags`]. Note that since Rust 1.55, `RUSTFLAGS` is removed from\nL385:   the environment; scripts should use `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` instead.\nL386: * `CARGO_PKG_<var>` --- The package information variables, with the same names and values as are [provided during crate building][variables set for crates].\nL387: [`tracing`]: https://docs.rs/tracing\nL388: [debug logging]: https://doc.crates.io/contrib/implementation/debugging.html#logging\nL389: [unix-like platforms]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#unix-and-windows\nL390: [windows-like platforms]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#unix-and-windows\nL391: [target family]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_family\nL392: [target operating system]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_os\nL393: [target architecture]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_arch\nL394: [target vendor]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_vendor\nL395: [target environment]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_env\nL396: [target ABI]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_abi\nL397: [pointer width]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_pointer_width\nL398: [target endianness]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_endian\nL399: [target features]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html#target_feature\nL400: [links]: build-scripts.md#the-links-manifest-key\nL401: [configuration]: ../../reference/conditional-compilation.html\n-------------------------